 (https://cot.mathres.org/issues/COT20253.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; The problem of characterizing SPN graphs is still not completely solved. ... - *any subdivision of K₄ in which more than one of the original edges was subdivided at least once,*\nThe latter was done by graph theoretic arguments, and some geometric arguments were used in the other proofs. This is due to the observation that a nonnegative Gram matrix of n vectors in ℝⁿ is completely positive if and only if these n vectors may be embedded in the nonnegative orthant of some ℝᵏ. This approach to complete positivity study was first used in [18], where such proofs were given for the results of [25]. Different proofs for some of the results of [24] used the Schur complement instead of geometric arguments [1, 14]. The term NLOC graphs (No Long Cycle Odd graphs) for these graphs was coined in [13].\n\n4. SPN GRAPHS\n\nThe problem of characterizing SPN graphs is still not completely solved. We present here the current state of the study. The existing results aim towards a characterization of these graphs both in terms of forbidden subgraphs, and in terms of the possible blocks of such graphs. For both we need to recall that an edge subdivision of G is the graph operation where an edge {u, v} is replaced by a path of length 2 whose ends are u and v, and its inner vertex is a new addition to the vertex set of G. A graph is a subdivision of G if it is obtained from G by a finite sequence of edge subdivisions, and G is considered to be a subdivision of itself.\n\nThe following theorem presents the known forbidden subgraphs for an SPN graph.\n\n**Theorem 4.1.** *If G is an SPN graph, then G does not contain the following:*\n- *any subdivision of K₄ in which more than one of the original edges was subdivided at least once,*\n- *any subdivision of the fan F₅,*\n- *any subdivision of CD₆,*\n- *any subdivision of T₆.*\n\nFigure 3 shows all these forbidden subgraphs. In this figure, each line represents a path of length 1 or more. These are all 2-connected, as any forbidden subgraph has to have a forbidden 2-connected subgraph. Shown, from left to right: two subdivisions of K₄, then subdivisions of F₅, CD₆, T₆.\n\nThe next theorem presents the known possible blocks of an SPN graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopological generation and matrix models for quantum reflection groups - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2020.106982)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; One particular “minimal” representation of 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) that has been conjectured to be inner faithful is Banica-Nechita's universal flat representation [12], [7].\nIn the above, Δ :𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) →𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) ⊗𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) denotes the coproduct and Δ(𝑘)= (id⊗Δ(𝑘−1))∘Δ for all 𝑘 ≥2. In particular, this means that 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) faithfully embeds into a C^{⁎}-ultraproduct of the sequence of algebras (𝐵⊗𝑘)𝑘∈ℕ. One particular “minimal” representation of 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) that has been conjectured to be inner faithful is Banica-Nechita's universal flat representation [12], [7]. Here, the idea is to consider the simplest examples of non-classical quantum permutations: 𝑁 ×𝑁 bistochastic matrices 𝑃= (𝑃𝑖⁢𝑗)𝑖,𝑗 whose entries are rank-one projections in 𝑃𝑖⁢𝑗 ∈𝑀𝑁⁡(ℂ). Such quantum permutations are typically called “flat” matrix models for 𝑆+𝑁, and the collection 𝑋𝑁 ⊂𝑀𝑁⁡(𝑀𝑁⁡(ℂ)) of all such flat quantum permutations forms a compact space. Banica's universal flat representation then takes the form 𝜋:𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁)→𝐶⁡(𝑋𝑁,𝑀𝑁)=𝑀𝑁⁡(𝐶⁡(𝑋𝑁)),𝜋⁡(𝑢𝑖⁢𝑗)={𝑋𝑁∋𝑃↦𝑃𝑖⁢𝑗}. In this paper, we use modifications of our topological generation results to verify the conjectured inner faithfulness of the universal flat representation for almost all values of N (cf. Corollary 4.9).\n\nTheorem 1.3\n\nFor all 𝑁 ≤5 and 𝑁 ≥10, the universal flat matrix model 𝜋 :𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) →𝑀𝑁⁡(𝐶⁡(𝑋𝑁)) is inner faithful.\n\nIntuitively this result says that even though 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) 